KNOWNBUG
main.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ deleted through a call result: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
"delete argument must have offset zero" failed: the address of the most
derived object was attached to a delete-expression only when its operand
had no side effects, so `delete make()' passed the pointer to the Y
subobject to __delete.  The check is now built over a placeholder for the
operand, which goto conversion replaces by the operand's value after its
side effects; `delete ps[i++]' evaluates the operand once.
